  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.3 × loss over x, z from −2 to 2. It is steeper in x, so steps overshoot there first: above learning rate 0.5 the x swings grow until the run is diverged.",
    "Noise: random, centred on the true slope, size 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch b out of 64 points, so it is exactly zero at 64. Values come from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence.",
    "Orange arrow: the actual next step, −learning rate × noisy slope. Grey arrow: −learning rate × true slope. Both are drawn at their true length in bowl units (scale 1).",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or is marked diverged when the loss goes above 8 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. Off-bowl points are drawn at the bowl's edge, and the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Run plays 5 steps a second. Changing a setting mid-run affects the next steps. Each trail holds up to 40 steps plus the start point. With noise, a single step can raise the loss: it only falls on average."
  ]
}